*Workflow and models -* Following data preprocessing and feature extraction (described in Data Summary), three modeling approaches were evaluated: two 1D Convolutional Neural Network architectures and Random Forest classifiers. The first CNN consisted of three convolutional blocks (32→64→128 filters, kernel size 5) with batch normalization, max pooling, dropout regularization (0.3-0.4), and dense classification layers (256→128 units), totaling ~320,000 parameters. After poor performance, a simplified CNN was tested with only two convolutional blocks (16→32 filters, larger kernels of size 7 and 5) and a single 64-unit dense layer, reducing parameters by 98% to 5,157. Both CNNs were trained using Adam optimizer (learning rate 0.001), early stopping (patience 15-20 epochs), and class weighting to handle imbalance. The Random Forest baseline used 200 trees with max_depth=20, followed by systematic hyperparameter optimization via 5-fold cross-validated GridSearchCV across 648 configurations, testing n_estimators (100-500), max_depth (10-30/None), min_samples_split (2-10), min_samples_leaf (1-4), and max_features (sqrt/log2/None).

*Evaluation metrics -* Model performance was assessed using multiple metrics appropriate for multi-class classification. Primary metrics included accuracy (overall correct classification rate), weighted F1-score (harmonic mean of precision and recall, weighted by class support), and per-class precision and recall to identify class-specific strengths and weaknesses. Confusion matrices visualized misclassification patterns between classes. For the final tuned Random Forest model, feature importance analysis identified which ppm regions contributed most to classification decisions globally, while SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values provided per-prediction explanations showing how individual spectral features influenced specific classification outcomes. All models were evaluated on a held-out test set (15% of data) that remained untouched during training and hyperparameter tuning to provide unbiased performance estimates.